1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,746
Every cell in your body
carries the same DNA.

2
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:08,785
So why does a neuron behave
nothing like the cell beside it?

3
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:13,268
Because in any one cell, only part of
that DNA is ever switched on.

4
00:00:13,800 --> 00:00:16,896
Which genes are switched on is
set by the epigenome.

5
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:21,460
Two layers: histone modifications,
and methylation of the DNA.

6
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:25,073
Layer one: the histones that
DNA is wrapped around.

7
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:29,141
Chemical marks on their tails
decide which genes are read.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,469
Acetylation opens the DNA, so
the gene can be read.

9
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:39,533
Methylation of the histone packs it
tightly, and the gene stays silent.

10
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:44,399
To measure both marks together,
NEXUS built HisTLAND®.

11
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:49,194
It reads two histone marks from one
sample, in bulk or in single cells.

12
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:53,485
Layer two sits on the DNA
itself: methylation.

13
00:00:56,400 --> 00:00:59,234
To map it genome-wide,
NEXUS built MeTLAND®.

14
00:00:59,800 --> 00:01:03,796
It maps DNA methylation without
bisulfite or antibodies.

15
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:12,388
NEXUS Epigenomics, founded in Stockholm, builds kits
around published methods from academic labs.

16
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:14,146
Ready to run in your own lab.

17
00:01:15,800 --> 00:01:18,966
Two layers of the epigenome.
HisTLAND® and MeTLAND®.

18
00:01:22,800 --> 00:01:25,906
What is the question you are
solving in epigenetics?
